<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William Massey</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1856–1925</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William Massey was Prime Minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> from 1912 until his death in 1925. Leader of the Reform Party, which drew its support from farmers, he used special constables to break the waterfront strike of 1913, led the country through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> and signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a> for it. He was one of the longest-serving prime ministers in New Zealand's history.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-massey.jpg" alt="William Massey" width="320" height="447" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Harris &amp;amp; Ewing · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:William_Ferguson_Massey_1919.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
